WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.934 --> 00:00:02.102
-Our first guest tonight is

00:00:02.102 --> 00:00:04.170
an Emmy- and Golden Globe-
nominated actor

00:00:04.170 --> 00:00:06.071
you know from movies
like "A Beautiful Mind"

00:00:06.071 --> 00:00:08.608
and as Vision in the
Marvel Cinematic Universe.

00:00:08.608 --> 00:00:10.977
He stars
in "A Very British Scandal,"

00:00:10.977 --> 00:00:12.779
which is streaming now
on Prime Video.

00:00:12.779 --> 00:00:14.080
Let's take a look.

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:14.914
[ Cheering and applause ]
-I'm sorry, but I just --

00:00:14.914 --> 00:00:15.881
I don't like it.

00:00:15.881 --> 00:00:17.049
-I can't put you down.

00:00:17.049 --> 00:00:17.917
Everybody's looking.

00:00:17.917 --> 00:00:18.951
-Oh, no,
you're going to drop me.

00:00:18.951 --> 00:00:20.153
-I'm not going
to [bleep] drop you.

00:00:20.153 --> 00:00:21.921
-Look, will you please
put me down. Please.

00:00:22.889 --> 00:00:27.126 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:27.126 --> 00:00:27.960
Ian.

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:28.728
[ Grunts ]

00:00:28.728 --> 00:00:33.566 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:33.566 --> 00:00:35.568
-There, you're down.

00:00:35.568 --> 00:00:37.437
Happy?

00:00:37.437 --> 00:00:38.471
-Please welcome back
to the show

00:00:38.471 --> 00:00:39.838
Paul Bettany, everybody.

00:00:39.838 --> 00:00:42.642
[ Cheering and applause ]

00:00:42.642 --> 00:00:51.417 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:51.417 --> 00:00:55.788 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:55.788 --> 00:00:56.856
Welcome back!

00:00:56.856 --> 00:00:58.992
-Nice to be back.
-It had been a long time.

00:00:58.992 --> 00:01:00.793
-It has.
Why haven't you had me back?

00:01:00.793 --> 00:01:02.662
-Well, I was waiting for you
to do good work.

00:01:02.662 --> 00:01:04.096
[ Laughter ]

00:01:04.096 --> 00:01:06.800
And I really enjoyed
this show a great deal.

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:08.835
So this is based
on a real-life scandal,

00:01:08.835 --> 00:01:13.406
the Duke and Duchess of Argyll,
which sounds made up,

00:01:13.406 --> 00:01:15.742
[ Laughter ]
but in that really awesome
sort of British way,

00:01:15.742 --> 00:01:17.043
those were real people.

00:01:17.043 --> 00:01:18.544
-They really were, yeah.

00:01:18.544 --> 00:01:19.645
And he was dreadful.

00:01:19.645 --> 00:01:21.681
-Yeah, he was a real bad guy.

00:01:21.681 --> 00:01:23.983
And you really own it, yeah.

00:01:23.983 --> 00:01:26.285
[ Laughter ]
-Thank you very much.
Yeah, it was disturbing,

00:01:26.285 --> 00:01:28.521
how quickly I fell
into character.

00:01:28.521 --> 00:01:30.290
-[ Laughs ]
-It was really nice.

00:01:30.290 --> 00:01:32.892
It was a nice palate cleanser
because, you know,

00:01:32.892 --> 00:01:35.594
Vision is sort of
warmth personified.

00:01:35.594 --> 00:01:37.096
-Yeah.
-It was nice to go

00:01:37.096 --> 00:01:39.732
and play a little, you know.
bit of an ass[bleep].

00:01:39.732 --> 00:01:41.968
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah, he's a full [bleep]heel.

00:01:41.968 --> 00:01:44.470
That was a really good clip
because it gives you

00:01:44.470 --> 00:01:47.139
a good sense of how the marriage
is just off to a bad start

00:01:47.139 --> 00:01:48.507
right from the gun.

00:01:48.507 --> 00:01:49.742
-Right off the bat.

00:01:49.742 --> 00:01:52.612
-Did you know much
about this scandal?

00:01:52.612 --> 00:01:55.080
-I didn't.
It sort of

00:01:55.080 --> 00:01:57.050
played out in the '50s and '60s

00:01:57.050 --> 00:01:58.918
and it's going to be hard
for you to believe,

00:01:58.918 --> 00:02:01.621
but it was slightly
before my time.

00:02:01.621 --> 00:02:03.723
[ Laughter ]
-No, I believe that, yeah.

00:02:03.723 --> 00:02:06.392
-Yeah. So, no, I didn't.
I didn't know anything about it.

00:02:06.392 --> 00:02:09.796
-And I guess that speaks to this
thing that I've often thought,

00:02:09.796 --> 00:02:12.665
which is maybe I think
us Americans assume

00:02:12.665 --> 00:02:15.401
that every British person
is deeply invested

00:02:15.401 --> 00:02:16.903
in sort of the royal family

00:02:16.903 --> 00:02:18.938
and its offshoots,
but that's maybe not the case.

00:02:18.938 --> 00:02:21.940
-Well, I think because you don't
have to pay for it...

00:02:21.940 --> 00:02:23.176
-Yeah, that's true.
-you know?

00:02:23.176 --> 00:02:24.510
[ Laughter ]

00:02:24.510 --> 00:02:26.178
-You have a resentment
about having to pay for it.

00:02:26.178 --> 00:02:28.013
-Every time those kids
get married,

00:02:28.013 --> 00:02:31.617
it's like £36 million
of taxpayers' dollars.

00:02:31.617 --> 00:02:33.052
[ Laughter ]
-And you can see it
on the tax bill?

00:02:33.052 --> 00:02:34.386
-Yeah.

00:02:34.386 --> 00:02:36.155
Well, what you get is
you get to watch

00:02:36.155 --> 00:02:38.724
some medieval procession that
you don't really understand

00:02:38.724 --> 00:02:41.427
going on for £36 million.

00:02:41.427 --> 00:02:43.229
-Claire Foy was in "The Crown."

00:02:43.229 --> 00:02:44.897
-She was, yeah.
-Wonderful in "The Crown."

00:02:44.897 --> 00:02:46.399
-Very great.
-It's wonderful
to see her in this.

00:02:46.399 --> 00:02:49.068
You weren't in "The Crown."

00:02:49.068 --> 00:02:50.402
[ Laughter ]
-[Both] Yeah.

00:02:50.402 --> 00:02:51.303
-You're 100% right.

00:02:51.303 --> 00:02:52.905
I'm like the only British actor

00:02:52.905 --> 00:02:54.440
that hasn't been in "The Crown."
-Yeah, that's what

00:02:54.440 --> 00:02:55.941
I was kind of getting at.
-Or "Harry Potter."

00:02:55.941 --> 00:02:57.443
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:57.443 --> 00:02:59.746
-Truth is, Seth, I'm actually

00:02:59.746 --> 00:03:01.714
from Hoboken.
-Oh, wow!

00:03:01.714 --> 00:03:03.382
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah, and I just do this
for work.

00:03:03.382 --> 00:03:05.185
-It's really good.
-Thank you very much.

00:03:05.185 --> 00:03:06.619
-I mean, for somebody
from Hoboken,

00:03:06.619 --> 00:03:09.521
it's very, very convincing.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:09.521 --> 00:03:11.357
Your parents were actors.

00:03:11.357 --> 00:03:13.125
-They were, yeah.
-Was this something that,

00:03:13.125 --> 00:03:14.360
from a very young age,

00:03:14.360 --> 00:03:15.861
you thought you were going
to jump into?

00:03:15.861 --> 00:03:17.363
-No, I didn't want
to do it at all.

00:03:17.363 --> 00:03:19.932
-Because of of watching them

00:03:19.932 --> 00:03:21.567
or...?
-It's just a bit silly,

00:03:21.567 --> 00:03:23.502
getting dressed up
in other people's clothes.

00:03:23.502 --> 00:03:24.904
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah, yeah.

00:03:24.904 --> 00:03:28.374
You know? And so I wanted to be
a guitar player.

00:03:28.374 --> 00:03:31.277
I wanted to be a guitar player
and I just wasn't very good,

00:03:31.277 --> 00:03:34.913
so I sort of fell into acting?

00:03:34.913 --> 00:03:36.782
Yeah, that's how that happened.

00:03:36.782 --> 00:03:39.318
-So, how do you fall into it?
Does somebody just kind of say

00:03:39.318 --> 00:03:41.187
like, "Hey, you"?
-Well, I was living

00:03:41.187 --> 00:03:42.722
with these --
I had two roommates

00:03:42.722 --> 00:03:44.490
and I was a busker
on the streets

00:03:44.490 --> 00:03:47.559
and the two roommates
did a midnight flit

00:03:47.559 --> 00:03:48.861
and left me with the rent

00:03:48.861 --> 00:03:50.429
and the landlord
took my guitars.

00:03:50.429 --> 00:03:52.031
And so I sort of
rung my parents up and went,

00:03:52.031 --> 00:03:53.332
"I failed!"
And I thought,

00:03:53.332 --> 00:03:55.434
"What am I going to do
with my --

00:03:55.434 --> 00:03:56.736
I guess I'll do some acting."

00:03:56.736 --> 00:03:59.238
[ Laughter ]
That's really how it happened.

00:03:59.238 --> 00:04:01.240
-You have children now.

00:04:01.240 --> 00:04:03.142
Do you think you would
recommend acting to them?

00:04:03.142 --> 00:04:04.543
-God, no.
-Okay.

00:04:04.543 --> 00:04:05.678
[ Laughter ]
-No!

00:04:05.678 --> 00:04:07.380
-Are you afraid, though,
that they might?

00:04:07.380 --> 00:04:09.548
I mean, again,
you live an interesting life.

00:04:09.548 --> 00:04:10.549
Are you worried
they're going to see it

00:04:10.549 --> 00:04:12.051
and be like, "I want to do it"?

00:04:12.051 --> 00:04:14.519
-Well, I've got two --
I've got three kids.

00:04:14.519 --> 00:04:16.989
Two, I've put off acting.

00:04:16.989 --> 00:04:18.490
I've got one left
to put off acting.

00:04:18.490 --> 00:04:23.195
My eldest child is
an aeronautical engineer.

00:04:23.195 --> 00:04:24.364
-Really?!
-Yeah.

00:04:24.364 --> 00:04:26.465
He's not biologically mine,
which is why he's

00:04:26.465 --> 00:04:28.835
[ Laughter ]
an aeronautical engineer,

00:04:28.835 --> 00:04:33.139
because he wasn't encumbered
with my math genes.

00:04:33.139 --> 00:04:35.407
I haven't understood a word
he said to me since he was ten.

00:04:35.407 --> 00:04:36.843
[ Laughter ]
-Really?
-Yeah.

00:04:36.843 --> 00:04:39.778
-So he's that far advanced
from you in maths and sciences?

00:04:39.778 --> 00:04:41.314
-No idea.
-Yeah.

00:04:41.314 --> 00:04:42.748
That's got to be hard.
[ Laughs ]

00:04:42.748 --> 00:04:44.316
-Not really
because there's my wife.

00:04:44.316 --> 00:04:45.618
-Yeah, yeah, that's right.
-Because my wife.

00:04:45.618 --> 00:04:47.053
Because of my accent,
my wife thought

00:04:47.053 --> 00:04:48.621
I was super clever and stuff.

00:04:48.621 --> 00:04:50.289
[ Laughter ]

00:04:50.289 --> 00:04:52.158
Because you get away with it,
with a British accent,

00:04:52.158 --> 00:04:56.329
and she actually went to Yale,
initially, to study physics

00:04:56.329 --> 00:04:59.799
and she met me and she thought
I was really smart

00:04:59.799 --> 00:05:02.167
and I actually left school
at 16.

00:05:02.167 --> 00:05:04.303
[ Laughter ]
And it was just awful,

00:05:04.303 --> 00:05:06.906
watching the light die
in her eyes as she...

00:05:06.906 --> 00:05:08.073
[ Laughter ]

00:05:08.073 --> 00:05:10.376
...realized actually how deeply

00:05:10.376 --> 00:05:13.479
[ Laughter ]
stupid and ill-educated I am.

00:05:13.479 --> 00:05:14.981
-Before dinner parties,
does she tell you like,

00:05:14.981 --> 00:05:16.549
"Just maybe don't talk
too much"?

00:05:16.549 --> 00:05:19.084
-Yes! Yes.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:19.084 --> 00:05:20.352
-"Let other people
do the talking."

00:05:20.352 --> 00:05:21.887
That British thing
where you're nodding.

00:05:21.887 --> 00:05:22.688
-That's exactly what I do.

00:05:22.688 --> 00:05:25.958
-Are your kids --

00:05:25.958 --> 00:05:27.059
Do they like --
For example,

00:05:27.059 --> 00:05:28.461
being in the
Marvel Cinematic Universe,

00:05:28.461 --> 00:05:29.962
is that something
that your kids think is cool,

00:05:29.962 --> 00:05:33.198
that their dad's an Avenger?

00:05:33.198 --> 00:05:36.635
-Yes, but they
torture me with it.

00:05:36.635 --> 00:05:39.171
My daughter has moved

00:05:39.171 --> 00:05:42.541
through all the different
Avengers as her favorite.

00:05:42.541 --> 00:05:44.377
It's never, never me.
-Yeah.

00:05:44.377 --> 00:05:46.045
[ Laughter ]

00:05:46.045 --> 00:05:48.881
And, actually, just recently,
I turned on --

00:05:48.881 --> 00:05:51.784
I wanted to watch "Moon Knight,"
which is fantastic.

00:05:51.784 --> 00:05:53.852
-A fantastic show,
yeah, with Oscar Ethan.

00:05:53.852 --> 00:05:56.622
-Exactly.
And I tried to log in

00:05:56.622 --> 00:06:02.394
to my Disney+ account
and they had edited my profile.

00:06:02.394 --> 00:06:03.563
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah.
-And I believe --

00:06:03.563 --> 00:06:05.231
-Yeah, we have it here.
So this is, again,

00:06:05.231 --> 00:06:06.632
one of your children did this?

00:06:06.632 --> 00:06:08.067
-Yeah, I have my suspicions,

00:06:08.067 --> 00:06:09.635
but I don't actually know who.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:09.635 --> 00:06:10.836
-This is very cruel.

00:06:10.836 --> 00:06:13.673
[ Laughter and applause ]
-I mean --

00:06:18.344 --> 00:06:21.213
Maybe you're all beginning
to build up some, you know,

00:06:21.213 --> 00:06:23.015
picture of my home life.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:23.015 --> 00:06:25.951
What I like is that
I feel like that's the face

00:06:25.951 --> 00:06:29.054
vision would make on the day
he found out he won that.

00:06:29.054 --> 00:06:30.255
[ Laughter ]
-It's not even me!

00:06:30.255 --> 00:06:32.091
It's a model of me.

00:06:32.091 --> 00:06:33.492
-They didn't even use you.

00:06:33.492 --> 00:06:35.994
[ Laughter ]
-They didn't even use me!

00:06:35.994 --> 00:06:37.996
-That must've been --

00:06:37.996 --> 00:06:39.365
You know, I got to talk
to Elizabeth about it.

00:06:39.365 --> 00:06:41.367
That must've been such --
"WandaVision" must've been

00:06:41.367 --> 00:06:42.701
an incredible show to do,
as an actor,

00:06:42.701 --> 00:06:46.038
just because it spanned
so many different genres.

00:06:46.038 --> 00:06:48.908
-Yeah, that was a real joy.

00:06:50.342 --> 00:06:53.112
We didn't feel any pressure
because we were supposed to come

00:06:53.112 --> 00:06:54.513
second out of the gate
and we thought

00:06:54.513 --> 00:06:55.814
we were going to be
the sort of kooky,

00:06:55.814 --> 00:06:59.385
niche show that, you know,
five people would watch,

00:06:59.385 --> 00:07:01.487
but those five people
would really love it.

00:07:01.487 --> 00:07:04.123
And then we came out first
and we kind of [bleep] ourselves

00:07:04.123 --> 00:07:06.291
[ Laughter ]
and then it went down
really well.

00:07:06.291 --> 00:07:09.195
But it was, it was so --

00:07:09.195 --> 00:07:10.863
it was so lovely.
I grew up watching

00:07:10.863 --> 00:07:12.130
those sitcoms, you know.

00:07:12.130 --> 00:07:13.866
I grew up watching
"The Dick Van Dyke Show."

00:07:13.866 --> 00:07:16.735
In London, in England,
they would play,

00:07:16.735 --> 00:07:19.104
you know, reruns
on Saturdays and Sundays.

00:07:19.104 --> 00:07:22.141
And I sort of had in my head,
"I know what these shows are.

00:07:22.141 --> 00:07:23.475
I know what Dick Van Dyke does."

00:07:23.475 --> 00:07:25.611
And then I went back to sort of,
you know, research them

00:07:25.611 --> 00:07:29.114
and they are brilliant.
He's brilliant.

00:07:29.114 --> 00:07:31.350
-They really are.
They're so well-choreographed,

00:07:31.350 --> 00:07:32.285
everything about it.

00:07:32.285 --> 00:07:33.886
-And they shot them in two days.

00:07:33.886 --> 00:07:35.087
I mean, it was extraordinary.

00:07:35.087 --> 00:07:37.223
-And you actually shot --
I guess the first episode

00:07:37.223 --> 00:07:39.258
was actually in front
of a live studio audience.

00:07:39.258 --> 00:07:41.359
-Yeah.
-And when was the last time

00:07:41.359 --> 00:07:43.929
you had performed in front
of a live studio audience?

00:07:43.929 --> 00:07:47.033
-So, 25 years prior to that
[ Laughter ]

00:07:47.033 --> 00:07:48.734
and I was really resistant.

00:07:48.734 --> 00:07:50.069
I really didn't want to do it

00:07:50.069 --> 00:07:52.204
and I said, "I think
this is a really bad idea."

00:07:52.204 --> 00:07:53.906
And they said, "Well,
we think it's the only way

00:07:53.906 --> 00:07:55.607
we can sort of get the energy
that they have

00:07:55.607 --> 00:07:58.276
when they're projecting past
the cameras to an audience."

00:07:58.276 --> 00:07:59.912
And I said, "Okay, okay."

00:07:59.912 --> 00:08:01.147
And I was so nervous

00:08:01.147 --> 00:08:03.749
and then I got on stage,
I got my first laugh,

00:08:03.749 --> 00:08:05.618
and I'm so shallow
that I was like,

00:08:05.618 --> 00:08:07.253
"I've wasted my entire life!

00:08:07.253 --> 00:08:10.322
[ Laughter ]
I should be doing sitcoms.

00:08:10.322 --> 00:08:12.024
What have I done?"
Yeah.

00:08:12.024 --> 00:08:14.193
-Well, it paid off.
I think we're all very happy

00:08:14.193 --> 00:08:15.527
you've managed to find a way
to do both

00:08:15.527 --> 00:08:16.729
and thanks so much
for being here.

00:08:16.729 --> 00:08:18.264
It's been too long.
Come back again soon.

00:08:18.264 --> 00:08:20.099
[ Cheering and applause ]
Paul Bettany, everybody.

00:08:20.099 --> 00:08:22.535
"A Very British Scandal" is
streaming now on Prime Video.

